/* Original instructional expansion, aligned to Microsoft SC-300 skills measured Oct 28, 2026. References: official guide and linked MicrosoftLearning lab index. No copied lab steps. */
window.__SC300_DEPTH_A=[[[2,1,"A directory role definition is a named collection of permitted directory actions. A role assignment binds that definition to a principal and supported scope. Built-in roles have Microsoft-defined permissions; custom roles use supported resource actions, subject to licensing. Neither is Azure resource RBAC.","Entra ID > Roles and administrators: inspect Application Administrator versus a narrowly scoped custom app role. In a fictional request, principal U1 needs only approved application operations. Examine role permissions, select supported scope, assign, and record assignment ID and status. Avoid Global Administrator.","Sign in as U1 in a separate authorized test session; perform the intended operation and test an unrelated privileged operation. If unexpectedly allowed, inspect additional direct/group/active roles.","An app support employee only needs one supported directory action. Which design minimizes exposure?","Choose a built-in least-privileged role or custom role with the required action and supported scope.","Grant Global Administrator and rely on training.","Use an Azure subscription Reader role."],[2,1,"An administrative unit (AU) is a management scope over selected directory objects, not an independent tenant or a permission grant. Use it when geographically or organizationally delegated administrators need supported administration of only specified objects.","Design AU-Houston with users Alex and Casey, and AU-Dallas with Morgan. Assign a supported User Administrator or Helpdesk role at Houston scope, subject to limitations on privileged users and supported actions. Document what tenant-wide permissions remain.","Compare an allowed Houston action to a denied Dallas action; inspect role scope and membership if both succeed. AU membership by itself does not grant resource access.","A regional team must reset eligible users' passwords only in Houston. What boundary belongs in the design?","Administrative unit scope plus an appropriate supported role.","A Microsoft 365 license for every Houston user.","A second domain name with no scoped role."],[2,1,"AU membership and role assignment are two separate records. A standard AU groups users/groups/devices; supported dynamic membership and restricted management AUs have distinct behavior and licensing. Choose only the features required.","Entra ID > Administrative units: create Houston Support; add approved objects; inspect its ID; add an eligible supported scoped administrator through Roles and administrators. If a dynamic rule is used, validate the resulting membership, not just the rule.","Read back membership, principal, role definition and directory scope. Test a supported action inside scope. If an object is missing, inspect membership evaluation; if access is broad, inspect other assignments.","An administrator was added as an AU member but has no delegated authority. What is missing?","An appropriate scoped administrative role assignment.","A new UPN suffix.","A device compliance policy."],[2,1,"Effective permissions combine applicable role assignments and supported scopes. An eligible PIM assignment is not active authority; inherited role-assignable group membership and other active roles may grant additional actions.","For U1, list direct role assignments, role-assignable groups and active PIM assignments. Example: Houston-scoped Helpdesk Administrator plus tenant-wide User Administrator. Determine the permission and scope of each action independently.","Use a separate test identity and allowed/denied operations. An unexpectedly allowed Dallas operation can come from a separate tenant-wide role; do not assume the narrow AU overrides it.","Why can U1 still manage Dallas users after receiving a Houston-scoped role?","An additional active or inherited assignment may grant tenant-wide access.","All AU roles always overwrite previous roles.","A verified domain grants administration."],[1,2,"A custom domain proves control over a DNS namespace so supported Microsoft services can use it. Verification does not automatically change existing UPNs, SMTP addresses or mail routing. A UPN is a login name, an SMTP address is for mail.","Entra ID > Domain names: add contoso.example, obtain the exact generated TXT or supported verification record, publish only in approved DNS, verify, then plan UPN suffix updates and application claims. In Microsoft 365, inspect required service records separately; MX routes mail.","Verify domain status and test a pilot user's login, mail and SSO after approved changes. If verification fails, check public authoritative DNS and exact token. Do not alter mail records merely to fix identity verification.","The new custom domain is verified. Does that prove Exchange mail is routed to Microsoft 365?","No; verify the separate Exchange service/MX configuration.","Yes, verification replaces all MX records.","Yes, if the user belongs to an AU."],[0,2,"Company branding customizes supported sign-in experiences; it is not identity proof. Default and localized branding may include logos, background, sign-in text and supported layout settings. Branding depends on feature/license and sign-in flow.","Entra ID > Company branding: choose an approved default logo, accessible colors, concise support text and language-specific variants where supported. Example: en-US and es-US sign-in text. Preview before publishing; do not expose sensitive internal contact data.","Test normal and localized login in a private session; verify official sign-in URL and authentication still works. If a logo does not show, inspect selected language, sign-in flow, image constraints and caching.","A branded login page looks like Contoso. Is appearance proof that it is legitimate?","No; validate the actual domain and authentication flow.","Yes, matching colors prove trust.","Yes, branding is equivalent to MFA."],[0,2,"Tenant properties describe organization metadata. User settings govern defaults such as allowed app registrations; group settings govern supported group creation/ownership behaviors; device settings govern join/registration limits and allowed populations. Defaults are distinct from per-object assignments.","Inventory current settings before changes. Example: tenant T1, app registrations restricted, approved group creators, device join allowed to pilot group. Use the appropriate Entra properties/settings pages, change one approved setting in isolation, keep rollback values.","Test a standard pilot user before and after, and verify admin operations still work. If an action still succeeds, inspect privileges and settings applying to that object; document exceptions.","A non-admin can create an app even after a policy change. What should be examined?","Effective app-registration settings and the user's actual roles/permissions.","Their display name.","The tenant's logo size."],[0,1,"A user object holds account identity, UPN, stable object ID, type, enabled status and descriptive properties. Creating it does not automatically assign app access. A synchronized user's authoritative attributes may need to be edited at the source.","Create fictional Alex with UPN alex@contoso.example, display name Alex Morgan, accountEnabled true, department Helpdesk, usageLocation US. Review generated ID U1, then assign only approved groups/licensing. Disable instead of prematurely deleting during offboarding; authorized deleted-user recovery is time limited.","Read user ID, attributes and effective access; verify new sign-in and an unauthorized resource. If updates revert, check source of authority/sync. After approved removal, validate actual denial and retention obligations.","Alex's UPN changes. Which field is generally the durable identifier for the same directory object?","Object ID.","Display name.","Department."],[1,3,"A security group collects principals for access; a Microsoft 365 group also supports collaboration resources. Membership may be assigned or dynamic. A dynamic rule evaluates user/device attributes; group membership and downstream authorization are separate.","Create fictional Finance Readers as security group. Assigned member Casey; alternatively use a supported dynamic rule such as user.department -eq \"Finance\". Verify group type, membership type, owner, actual membership and app assignment; use role-assignable groups only where explicitly appropriate.","After Alex transfers, inspect evaluated membership and any direct grants, then test Finance and Helpdesk access. Delay or rule error can make actual membership differ from expected. Nested/group behavior varies by feature.","A dynamic rule was saved but Casey has not appeared in the group yet. What establishes entitlement?","Actual evaluated membership plus the resource assignment and access result.","The rule text alone.","The tenant subscription ID."],[0,null,"Custom security attributes are governed key/value metadata for Entra objects and supported authorization scenarios. They are separate from ordinary profile attributes; role permissions to define and assign them are deliberately separated.","Define attribute set BusinessClassification, attribute CostCenter (string, allowed values FIN/IT); use appropriate attribute definition and assignment roles at the supported scope. Assign IT to approved fictional principal. Do not store passwords or unnecessary sensitive data.","Read back value using an appropriately authorized identity; test an unauthorized reader/writer. If an administrator cannot see the values, review attribute-specific role and set scope, not merely User Administrator.","A User Administrator cannot assign a governed custom security attribute. What should be examined?","Attribute assignment role and attribute-set scope.","Add every employee as Global Administrator.","Change the UPN to the attribute name."],[1,1,"Bulk operations transform many records from an approved input set. CSV has headers and rows; PowerShell emits objects through a pipeline. A preview identifies invalid UPNs, duplicate IDs and missing required fields before any write.","Download the current bulk template rather than inventing headers. In a fictional CSV, check Alex, Casey, Sam records for unique sign-in names. Preview Import-Csv, inspect Get-MgContext, read matching users with Get-MgUser -All -Property Id,UserPrincipalName; use the authorized Graph cmdlet only after approval.","Inspect per-row failures, compare intended IDs to actual records, and record rollback for successful writes. Do not run broad write commands against a live tenant just to learn.","Five rows failed and five succeeded. What should a safe retry do?","Inspect individual errors and retry only corrected failed rows after checking current state.","Blindly rerun every write.","Suppress the error output."],[0,10,"A cloud device object represents a device identity. Registered, Entra joined and hybrid joined are different relationships. Windows flags and cloud DeviceId connect endpoint evidence to a directory object; enrollment is a separate management relationship.","On approved Windows client inspect dsregcmd /status. Example: DomainJoined YES, AzureAdJoined YES = hybrid joined in this context; DeviceId D1 matches Entra Devices. Compare a registered BYOD record, cloud joined record and hybrid joined record. Check status, owner, enabled and stale records.","Check matching device ID and join state in sign-in details. If a device is duplicated, investigate the actual signed-in device and enrollment before deleting any record. Join alone does not prove compliant.","DomainJoined=YES and AzureAdJoined=YES. Which relationship is indicated?","Microsoft Entra hybrid join, subject to matching device evidence.","Intune compliance by definition.","Entra registered BYOD only."],[0,3,"A product SKU contains service plans. A user can receive direct or group-based product licensing; usage location and available seats/service plan conflicts affect assignment. A license enables a feature, not private resource authorization.","In authorized Microsoft 365 admin center inspect Billing > Licenses. Example: Helpdesk group gets approved SKU, user Alex usageLocation US, selected plans enabled. Compare direct and inherited assignment sources; do not buy seats or activate trials. Review available existing entitlements.","Inspect license assignment errors and assigned plans, then test relevant workload. Removing a group license might leave a direct assignment. Missing usage location or exhausted seats needs correction, not a broader role.","An employee is in a licensed group but has a licensing error. What evidence is relevant?","Usage location, available seats, service-plan conflicts and assignment error.","The SAML NameID alone.","The Azure VM's managed identity."],[2,4,"External collaboration settings regulate who may invite guests, guest visibility of directory objects and supported domain restrictions. They do not replace cross-tenant access or resource permissions.","External Identities > External collaboration settings: design approved inviter roles, limited guest visibility and supported domain allow/deny rule. Example: authorized sponsors may invite partner.example; unapproved domain is denied. Evaluate email OTP/self-service only where required.","Test approved inviter/domain and unauthorized inviter/domain. Inspect guest directory visibility and resource entitlement separately. Settings can affect business collaboration; record rollback before changing.","A guest invitation is allowed. Does that mean the guest may open all SharePoint documents?","No; resource assignments and sharing/access policy remain separate.","Yes, guest invitation is full resource consent.","Only if their UPN includes #EXT#."],[2,5,"Invitation creates or refers to a local external user representation and delivers a redemption path. Invitation state indicates whether the guest has accepted; it does not guarantee access to a specific app. Bulk invitations use the current CSV template.","Create a fictional invitation for casey@partner.example with approved redirect, sponsor and message; add only approved project assignment after identity validation. For bulk, validate email addresses, duplicate entries and per-row errors against current template.","Check external user object, invitation/redemption status, assigned resources and actual sign-in. If guest cannot redeem, inspect identity provider, invite address, cross-tenant policy and invitation state before reinviting.","A guest has PendingAcceptance state. What is not yet demonstrated?","Successful redemption and target resource access.","Existence of any invitation record.","The tenant's verified domain."],[2,24,"An external user may persist after a project ends; guest lifecycle includes sponsor, access assignment, review, expiry, disable/delete according to approved retention policy. B2B user type alone does not identify every access path.","Inspect external user U-G1: home identity, sponsor, groups, enterprise app roles, packages and direct resource grants. After contract expiry, remove approved access, revoke sessions/disable or delete per policy; avoid removing unrelated collaboration that remains authorized.","Verify access denied at actual project resource and review alternate direct/group grants. Check guest account state, sign-in evidence and data retention, not just package expiration.","The guest's package expired but direct project membership remains. What should be done?","Review and remove approved obsolete direct and inherited access paths.","Assume expiry removed all permissions.","Reset home tenant administrator password."],[2,null,"Cross-tenant access settings control B2B collaboration/direct connect inbound and outbound relationships. Inbound concerns external identities accessing your resources; outbound concerns your identities accessing another organization. Supported trust settings can accept external MFA or device claims.","External Identities > Cross-tenant access settings: inspect default and organization-specific settings for Partner T2. Plan inbound pilot users/apps and outbound access; enable trust of MFA/compliant/hybrid claims only with approved trust assessment. Distinguish B2B direct connect and collaboration.","Validate an allowed and denied inbound case, and an outbound case. Inspect sign-in details, home/resource tenant IDs and trust settings; check resource permissions separately.","Contoso users must access Partner resources. Which Contoso direction applies?","Outbound access from Contoso, alongside Partner inbound policy.","Contoso inbound only.","Microsoft 365 license assignment only."],[2,null,"Cross-tenant synchronization provisions supported B2B users from source to target with mappings and scope; it is not a merger of directories or a replacement for cross-tenant sign-in policies. Target users have tenant-local object IDs.","Design Source T1 > Target T2 with approved inbound synchronization setup and supported automatic redemption settings. Configure scoped source assignments and attribute mappings, test on-demand provisioning for Alex, then monitor create/update/deprovision and provisioning logs.","Verify source U1 maps to target U9; check role/entitlement in target independently. If no target record, inspect source scope, target permission, mapping and provisioning errors; if sign-in denied inspect cross-tenant and resource access.","Synchronization created U9 in Target T2. Does that prove Alex can read target Payroll?","No; target authorization and applicable access policy must be satisfied.","Yes, synchronized identities are tenant admins.","Yes, source object ID automatically grants access."],[3,6,"External federation lets a supported external identity provider authenticate a user through a trust relationship. SAML or WS-Fed conveys signed assertions/tokens under configured issuer, endpoints and certificate trust; local guest/resource access still applies.","External Identities > identity providers: identify partner domain, protocol, issuer/entity ID, passive sign-in endpoint and signing certificate/metadata. Configure only supported verified domain/trust arrangement in authorized lab; invite a scoped test user and check claim mapping.","Validate successful redemption, issuer/signature and a permitted resource. Expired certificate, wrong issuer or mismatched claim can break federation even when directory records exist. Use approved certificate rollover and fallback procedure.","Partner changes signing certificate and federated logins fail. What is the first relevant trust check?","Configured issuer, metadata/signing certificate and actual assertion validation.","Group license seat count.","Intune encryption policy."],[0,7,"Connect Sync uses a synchronization engine with connectors for source and target, import, synchronization rules/metaverse and export. The source anchor links corresponding identities. Scope and mapping determine which attributes are exported.","In a disposable approved AD/Entra lab, choose supported Connect version, inventory domains and verified UPN suffixes, scope pilot OU, choose source anchor and authentication method. Review import/sync/export result and staging-mode plan; never install on production for practice.","Compare source AD record, connector-space/metaverse evidence where applicable and cloud object. If export fails, investigate duplicate attributes, unverified suffix or permission; if import missing, inspect source scope and replica.","An AD user exists but connector import never sees it. Which evidence comes first?","Source replica and connector scope/import results.","Cloud app consent.","A PIM activation request."],[0,null,"Cloud Sync uses a cloud-managed provisioning configuration and lightweight on-premises agents. It differs from Connect Sync in supported objects, topology, features and administration; check current feature matrix before selecting.","Plan agent placement and redundancy, register approved agents in a training environment, create scoped configuration, define mapping and test a selected user using supported on-demand provisioning. Avoid overlapping conflicting sync scopes.","Inspect agent health, provisioning logs, source scope and target object. If the agent is healthy but target record absent, check scope/mapping and specific provisioning event rather than reinstalling blindly.","An agent is healthy but a user outside the selected scope never appears in Entra. What is likely?","Scope exclusion, not proof of an agent outage.","App registration consent failure.","Missing Conditional Access session frequency."],[1,7,"Password hash synchronization (PHS) synchronizes a further-derived representation of the AD password hash; cloud validation does not require a live on-premises controller for each new cloud password sign-in. It does not copy plaintext passwords and is distinct from object synchronization.","Select supported PHS configuration in approved hybrid lab, ensure pilot user is synchronized and password changes flow, then test a cloud sign-in. Compare object sync status with password sync evidence; do not attempt to extract actual hashes.","If user object exists but new password fails, inspect password sync health, freshness and account state plus policy. PHS cloud validation can continue during an on-prem outage when other cloud controls pass.","Does PHS require a PTA agent to validate every cloud password sign-in?","No; current synchronized derived hash enables cloud validation.","Yes, PTA is mandatory.","Yes, OAuth requires an on-prem password."],[1,null,"Pass-through authentication (PTA) forwards a protected validation request through an available on-premises agent to AD. The agent validates credentials against AD; cloud policy then applies. Agent health and AD connectivity are key dependencies.","Choose PTA for approved requirements, deploy supported agents with redundancy, confirm Entra configuration and agent status, test pilot cloud login and approved failure/recovery behavior. PHS may be configured separately as a planned recovery option, not automatic failover by default.","Inspect agent availability and on-prem AD validation errors plus sign-in details. If all agents are unavailable, new PTA password validation may fail. Test a supported recovery strategy rather than relying on assumptions.","All PTA agents are unavailable. What does a working cloud user object prove?","The object exists, but new PTA password validation may still fail.","The password is always cloud-validated.","All sign-in policies are bypassed."],[1,null,"Seamless SSO is an optional experience using Kerberos for eligible domain-connected browsers under supported PHS/PTA deployments. It reduces prompts; it is not PHS, PTA or the Primary Refresh Token (PRT).","In approved lab confirm supported client, corporate network/domain connectivity, service principal/AD computer account configuration and browser zone settings per current docs. Test domain user navigating to a supported Microsoft sign-in without unnecessary prompts.","If password sign-in works but seamless SSO does not, inspect client domain connectivity, Kerberos ticket and browser configuration. Modern Entra joined devices may use PRT-based SSO instead.","A domain-connected user can authenticate with PHS but sees a prompt. Which is a plausible separate issue?","Seamless SSO Kerberos/browser path.","PHS must have deleted the object.","A license automatically prevents prompts."],[1,null,"AD FS federation delegates authentication to an external federation service. Moving toward managed authentication changes trust and validation dependencies; apps, claims, MFA and recovery must be assessed before changing domains.","Inventory federated domains, apps relying on AD FS claims and authentication rules; select managed authentication method; establish cloud emergency access; use supported staged rollout for eligible pilot users; validate business apps and document rollback before converting approved domains.","Inspect sign-in method, token claims and app compatibility; monitor old/new paths and failures. Do not retire federation infrastructure until approved migration and dependencies are verified.","What must precede conversion of a production federated domain?","Pilot validation, claims/application dependency inventory and recovery/rollback plan.","Deleting every federated app.","Assigning all users Global Administrator."],[2,null,"Connect Health provides supported health monitoring for hybrid identity components. An alert identifies a monitored condition; it is evidence, not proof that it caused every user's failure.","Review supported Connect Health views/agents for the relevant sync, AD DS or AD FS deployment; inspect agent last contact, service health and specific alert. Correlate timestamps with import/export or sign-in failure; validate licensing and supported agent.","After approved remediation, confirm health signal returns and original operation succeeds. If health is green but export still fails, inspect detailed provisioning evidence; do not treat dashboard color as end-to-end validation.","Connect Health alert is cleared but Alex still fails synchronization. What is next?","Investigate the specific sync import/export and source object evidence.","Close ticket because alert cleared.","Disable directory audits."]],[[2,8,"Authentication planning matches proof methods to threat, population, device and recovery needs. Authentication strength limits acceptable combinations; method availability is not the same as a requirement. Recovery and emergency access are independent design decisions.","Define workforce, admins, contractors and recovery groups. Example: administrators use phishing-resistant methods; supported pilot workforce uses Authenticator/passkey; approved bootstrap TAP. Inventory registration and licenses, rollout in pilot, document recovery and excluded emergency identities.","Test allowed proof, disallowed weak proof, onboarding/recovery and approved break-glass procedure. Check authentication details and CA result, not only method registration.","Which design proves admins must use phishing-resistant proof?","A supported authentication-strength requirement targeted to admins, with approved methods and validated sign-ins.","Only enable Authenticator for everyone.","Only add users to an app group."],[2,8,"Authenticator may provide push/number matching and supported passwordless flows. Passkeys use FIDO public-key challenge response and origin binding. TAP is a time-limited bootstrap/recovery credential. Certificate-based authentication validates approved X.509 certificate trust and mapping. These are different proof mechanisms.","In Authentication methods policy target pilot groups per method; configure supported passkey/authenticator options, TAP issuance lifetime and use restrictions, and CBA trusted issuer/binding settings where authorized. Have test identities register supported methods and compare sign-in proof.","Inspect method registration and authentication details; test allowed strength and disallowed method. Do not publish TAPs, private keys, certificates with secrets or passwords. Method availability alone does not enforce MFA.","What property makes a passkey phishing resistant in its supported flow?","Challenge signing and binding to the legitimate service origin.","Sending the private key to the web server.","Reusing a six-digit static password."],[2,8,"Tenant-wide MFA behavior can involve security defaults, legacy per-user settings, and Conditional Access; authentication methods policy controls availability. Avoid conflicting or redundant configuration and do not disable existing security controls without approved replacement.","Inventory current methods, security defaults/per-user MFA status and CA policies. For modern supported design, target pilot group and app with CA MFA or authentication strength, exclude approved emergency recovery accounts as appropriate, use report-only before enforcement.","Check actual authentication details and all matching policy results. Existing registration is not proof of enforced prompt; existing session proof can satisfy requirements depending on context. Validate approved recovery path.","A method is enabled tenant-wide. Does every sign-in necessarily require that method?","No; method eligibility and access-policy enforcement are separate.","Yes, enabled methods always force MFA.","Yes, provided a custom domain exists."],[2,9,"Self-service password reset (SSPR) lets eligible users recover passwords using configured verification methods. For synchronized users, supported password writeback can update the on-premises authority; it depends on configuration, permissions and licensing.","Entra password reset settings: select approved pilot group, supported recovery methods/count, registration and notification settings. Where needed plan writeback via supported hybrid configuration. Register test user and perform approved recovery in separate session.","Verify user can authenticate with updated password at intended systems; inspect reset and writeback events. If cloud reset succeeds but AD does not update, check writeback support/agent permissions and source policy.","A hybrid user's cloud recovery must change the on-premises password. What feature needs explicit configuration?","Supported password writeback.","A new app role.","A My Apps collection."],[2,null,"Windows Hello for Business uses asymmetric keys bound to supported user/device enrollment; PIN or biometric unlocks use of the local key rather than sending the PIN to the service. Trust model and provisioning prerequisites depend on cloud/hybrid design.","Plan cloud Kerberos trust or another supported trust model as appropriate, verify device join and management, configure approved WHfB policy (often through Intune), ensure prerequisite MFA and enrollment, provision pilot and test sign-in/resource scenarios.","Inspect dsregcmd /status and relevant provisioning/sign-in logs; confirm expected key/SSO behavior and recovery. A local PIN failure is different from a remote password failure.","Does a Hello PIN normally travel to Entra as the shared cloud password?","No; it authorizes local use of a device-bound credential.","Yes, it is the user's UPN.","Yes, it is a SAML assertion."],[2,null,"Disabling account blocks new authentication as supported; revoking sign-in sessions invalidates supported refresh/session artifacts. Existing access tokens may remain usable until expiry or supported CAE reevaluation. Resource grants and retention are separate.","For approved leaver, identify source authority, disable account (on-prem if synchronized as required), synchronize, use supported revoke sessions operation, remove obsolete entitlements and deprovision targets. Record exact user ID and approval; avoid deleting retained data automatically.","Verify denied new sign-in and resource outcomes, inspect sign-in and audit logs and supported token/session behavior. Existing resource sessions may require additional service-specific action.","Why is disabling an account not the same as immediate universal termination of all existing sessions?","Token lifetime, CAE/client/resource support and service-specific session handling differ.","Disabling a user only changes their display name.","All sessions are controlled by DNS."],[2,12,"Entra password protection rejects weak/banned passwords using Microsoft global banned terms and supported custom lists. Smart lockout limits repeated failed authentication behavior; it is not the same as password complexity. On-prem protection requires supported agents/configuration.","Plan custom banned terms reflecting organizational risks, choose approved enforcement or audit approach for on-prem extension, review smart-lockout configuration and AD lockout interaction. Test a fictional banned password in authorized isolated lab only; never probe live accounts repeatedly.","Review password change/reset or agent logs, and distinguish banned-password rejection from lockout. Avoid weakening thresholds to bypass an incident; inspect source policy.","Which feature prevents selection of an organization-specific weak password?","Custom banned-password protection, not merely smart-lockout duration.","App collection.","A service principal display name."],[1,null,"Microsoft Entra Kerberos provides supported Kerberos authentication for particular hybrid/resource scenarios, such as cloud Kerberos trust or supported Azure Files configurations. It is not a universal replacement for traditional domain controllers or PHS.","Choose a supported scenario and read its current prerequisites. In a training design identify AD domain, Entra Kerberos server object/trust, client and resource, permitted identities and resource permissions. For WHfB cloud trust, validate relevant hybrid user and DC reachability as required.","Inspect relevant ticket evidence with approved tools and test actual resource access separately. If a Kerberos ticket exists but file access denied, inspect share/data ACL; if ticket missing, check trust and client prerequisites.","A user obtains a supported Kerberos ticket but cannot read a share. What remains?","Resource-level permissions and applicable access rules.","Convert all groups to Microsoft 365 groups.","Disable token validation."],[0,13,"Conditional Access evaluates targeted identities, resources and conditions against grant and session controls. Design starts with a business rule, threat and measurable intended behavior; policies can combine and block takes precedence over grants.","Write requirement: Finance pilot, Payroll app, require approved phishing-resistant authentication and compliant device; specify exclusions and emergency recovery, report-only evaluation, rollback owner and success/denial tests. Map subject, resource, condition, control and state before creating.","Review What If plus actual sign-in logs and report-only results. Verify allowed and denied scenarios with no unexpected broad impact. Only authorized rollout moves to On.","What is the safest first deployment of a new broad access requirement?","Scoped report-only pilot, evidence review and approved enforcement.","Immediately turn On for all users.","Disable all existing controls."],[0,13,"Assignments determine where a CA policy applies: users/groups or supported workload identities, target resources/actions and selected conditions. Exclusions remove scope for that policy only; another policy may still apply.","Create fictional CA-Finance-Pilot, include FinancePilot, target Payroll, inspect device/client/location conditions, exclude only justified emergency identities. Test Alex in group and Morgan outside. Workload policies have different supported controls.","In What If and sign-in logs inspect Applied/Not applied and reason. If policy does not apply, check group membership, target resource and conditions. A successful sign-in does not by itself prove exclusion.","Alex is not in FinancePilot and no other matching scope exists. What should this pilot policy do?","Not apply to Alex.","Grant Finance app permission automatically.","Always block the entire tenant."],[0,13,"Grant controls decide whether access is blocked or requires supported proof, such as MFA, strength or compliant device. Multiple controls can require all or one selected control; every applicable enforced policy must be satisfied.","Model CA-Finance with MFA AND compliant device. Alex MFA=true/compliant=false fails this grant; change to OR and it may meet that policy's grant, but other policies/resource authorization still matter. Do not change AND to OR just to hide compliance failures.","Run both combinations in simulator, inspect policy and individual control outcomes; test enforced block and report-only separately. If unexpectedly allowed, check policy state, scope and other conditions.","MFA passes, compliant device fails, enforced rule requires both. Expected outcome?","The grant requirements are not satisfied.","MFA overrides every device requirement.","App assignment automatically meets the grant."],[0,13,"What If predicts applicability for selected inputs; report-only provides observed evaluation without enforcing that policy; sign-in logs show actual event details and policy outcomes. None replaces testing a real approved operation.","Choose test user, app, client and device; evaluate What If; perform test sign-in in authorized pilot; inspect Conditional Access tab, correlation ID, auth details and device. Compare policy IDs and results with expected scope/control.","A mismatch requires checking actual resource, device signals, group evaluation and existing proof. Confirm original permitted action and denied case before enforcement. Record rollback and review report-only results over representative use.","What If predicts grant, but actual sign-in is blocked. What evidence is most useful?","Actual sign-in event, matching policies, conditions and device/authentication details.","Policy display color.","The tenant logo."],[2,null,"Session controls constrain supported post-authentication behavior. Sign-in frequency defines when reauthentication is required under applicable behavior; persistent browser session controls cookie/session persistence. App-enforced restrictions and CA App Control are different supported mechanisms.","Define business requirement for sensitive browser sessions. Scope test policy to approved group/app, choose supported frequency/persistence setting, and check interaction with client/session behavior. Avoid claiming every refresh triggers new MFA.","Test fresh browser, existing session, elapsed interval and applicable resource/client; inspect actual reauthentication and policy details. Do not infer universal token expiry from a sign-in-frequency setting.","What does sign-in frequency govern?","Reauthentication behavior under supported session policy, not app assignment.","The number of licenses assigned.","DNS TTL for the tenant."],[1,null,"Device-based access can use device identity and posture, while application-enforced restrictions pass relevant context to supported resources that enforce limited access. A managed/compliant device is distinct from registered or hybrid-joined device.","Plan supported unmanaged-browser access to SharePoint or Exchange with appropriate workload settings and CA session control, or require compliant device for full access. Compare allowed limited action and blocked download in approved lab; check Intune compliance for intended devices.","Inspect matching device ID, join/compliance signals, policy session result and resource-side behavior. If restriction not enforced, confirm client/resource support and workload configuration.","A device is joined but lacks compliance. Does join alone satisfy Require compliant device?","No; the relevant compliance signal must satisfy the grant.","Yes, join and compliance are synonyms.","Yes, if user has an E5 license."],[2,null,"Continuous Access Evaluation (CAE) allows supported resource/client combinations to react to certain critical events and location-policy changes without waiting for conventional token expiry. It does not guarantee every service instantly ends every session.","Map supported apps/clients, critical events and network policies. In authorized test, observe a session before and after a supported event such as account disable, then inspect subsequent resource challenge or denial. Do not simulate against live employees.","Verify resource and client support, sign-in/token behavior and timing. If an unsupported app keeps an existing session, use approved service-specific actions. CAE is not a universal revocation switch.","Why might an existing session survive an account change temporarily?","Unsupported CAE paths or token/session behavior may differ.","CAE removes all policies.","Every token ignores issuer."],[1,null,"Authentication context is a label an integrated app can request for a sensitive operation. A CA policy targets that context and specifies required proof. The app must request the context and validate the result; naming a context alone does nothing to an arbitrary button.","Define c1=PayrollExport, configure approved policy targeting c1 with strength, and have supported app request the required claims/context on export. Normal browse stays under baseline policy. Test export with and without acceptable proof.","Check app integration, context ID and sign-in policy result; inspect relevant token claim and app-side enforcement. Never infer sensitive action is protected solely because context exists.","An export succeeds without strong proof although context c1 exists. What should be checked?","Whether the app requests and validates c1 and the relevant CA policy applies.","Only the context's friendly name.","The user's Microsoft 365 theme."],[1,null,"Protected actions associate selected supported Entra permissions with authentication context so an administrator must satisfy additional proof to perform a sensitive operation. This does not grant the underlying permission.","Identify supported sensitive permission (for example an eligible policy-management action), create context and approved strength policy, associate action in protected actions settings with appropriate admin authorization, pilot before broader use.","Test an admin who has underlying permission but lacks required proof; test the same approved operation after satisfying context. Verify audit and sign-in results and recovery exclusions. Avoid locking out policy administrators.","Can a user perform a protected admin action merely by satisfying its authentication context?","No; the user must also have the underlying administrative permission.","Yes, context grants Global Admin.","Yes, if they own an app."],[0,13,"Conditional Access templates prepopulate recommended policy settings. They accelerate drafting, not organizational risk assessment; scope, exclusions and controls must be inspected before enabling.","Choose supported template relevant to business goal, review generated name, identities, target resources, exclusions, conditions, grant/session controls and state. Adapt to approved pilot and test report-only, then documented enforcement approval.","Check representative sign-in logs for unexpected impact and recovery path. Templates may change; record resulting actual policy configuration rather than trusting template title.","What should you do after generating a policy from a template?","Inspect all settings, scope and exclusions and validate pilot outcomes.","Enable immediately without review.","Delete every existing policy."],[1,14,"User risk represents likelihood the identity is compromised, aggregated from relevant detections. Risk-based access/remediation can use supported Entra ID Protection and CA, depending on licenses and configuration; user risk differs from a specific sign-in risk.","Review risky user record, detections and history. Design scoped policy for approved remediation or block at selected risk level; for password-based compromised users, use supported secure password-change/recovery conditions as appropriate. Check registration prerequisites.","Confirm risk disposition and actual approved recovery; investigate false positives using evidence. Do not dismiss risk merely to allow a sign-in. Verify new sign-in and monitor related activity.","A user remains high risk after one low-risk sign-in. Is that contradictory?","No; account-level risk and one event's sign-in risk are separate.","Yes, all sign-in events reset user risk.","Yes, risk is a licensing error."],[1,14,"Sign-in risk concerns probability that a specific authentication request is not legitimate. It is evaluated from supported detection signals and can trigger step-up or block depending on policy. It does not necessarily mean account compromise is confirmed.","Inspect risky sign-in details, time, user, resource, client and detection type. Plan pilot risk policy for specified levels, with approved proof/block outcome. Do not generate malicious sign-ins in a real tenant to practice.","Correlate actual event and policy result, confirm legitimate or compromised disposition according to approved investigation. Compare user-risk history separately.","An unfamiliar sign-in triggers risk, but no evidence yet establishes compromise. What is appropriate?","Investigate that event and apply approved risk-based controls.","Dismiss all risk detections.","Grant admin rights for verification."],[2,15,"Registration campaigns encourage or require eligible users to set up supported authentication methods during sign-in flows; method policies determine availability. Registering proof does not itself enforce it for every access request.","Authentication methods > Registration campaign: inspect supported target population, exclusions, method eligibility and snooze behavior. Pilot users lacking required registration, provide recovery/helpdesk process and communication. Use approved tenant settings only.","Check registration details and actual sign-in requirements; compare eligible unregistered user and registered user. If no prompt, inspect targeting, eligibility and existing methods.","A campaign finishes and a user registers Authenticator. What still determines whether MFA is required?","Applicable authentication and access policy.","The campaign name alone.","The user's SMTP alias."],[1,14,"Risk investigation is an evidence workflow: identify alert scope, inspect detections, correlated sign-ins, IP/client/resource and account changes; select approved remediation or confirmed-safe disposition. Risk state is not the same as an incident conclusion.","Fictional event E1 shows high sign-in risk; user U1 also has suspicious consent grant. Compare known baseline, audit changes and affected resources; contain approved access, secure credentials, revoke appropriate sessions, remove malicious grant through authorized incident procedure.","Record evidence, risk disposition, verified recovered access and follow-up monitoring. A reset without investigating app grants or workload persistence may leave other exposure.","Which action best follows a confirmed compromised account?","Approved containment/remediation plus verification of credentials, sessions and relevant grants.","Only rename the user.","Only dismiss the alert."],[0,null,"Workload identity risk concerns nonhuman principals and suspicious activity. A service principal can have credentials, permissions, owners and sign-in logs; a user password reset does not fix a compromised application credential.","Inspect risky workload identity, service principal sign-in evidence, credential additions and consent/audit history. Map app ID, principal ID, credential and permissions; rotate/revoke approved compromised credentials or remove unauthorized grants with service-owner coordination.","Test required workload operation using approved replacement credentials, confirm unauthorized operation denied and monitor further sign-ins. Avoid destroying production dependency without rollback.","A risky service principal is using a newly added secret. Which evidence/remediation is relevant?","Principal credentials and grants, audit/sign-in evidence and approved secret rotation.","Reset an unrelated employee password.","Change SharePoint branding."],[2,null,"Global Secure Access client acquires supported network traffic and forwards it according to assigned traffic profiles. Deployment is not simply enabling a CA policy; device support, client health and forwarding configuration matter.","Plan pilot users/devices, check current client prerequisites and licensing, deploy supported client under approved management, enable required forwarding profile and assignment, verify traffic acquisition and health. Do not purchase GSA licenses for practice.","Inspect client status/diagnostics and traffic logs for intended destinations; distinguish client disconnected, profile not assigned and app authorization denial.","Client is installed but traffic not acquired. What should be checked first?","Client health, forwarding profile, assignment and supported destination.","The user's company logo.","A PIM approval for every packet."],[2,null,"Entra Private Access provides identity-aware access to supported private applications through connectors and app segments. Connector reachability, destination host/port and assigned user/policy are separate requirements.","Design Payroll private host/port, deploy approved connector group with reachability and redundancy, configure supported Quick Access or per-app segment as appropriate, assign pilot users, apply suitable CA policy and client profile.","Validate private DNS/host/port path, connector health and authorized access; test denied unassigned user. If sign-in succeeds but target unreachable, check connector and segment rather than resetting password.","All users authenticate, but connector to private Payroll is down. What fails?","Private application connectivity path.","Tenant branding.","User licensing assignment only."],[2,null,"Entra Internet Access is a secure web gateway capability for supported internet traffic. Forwarding, filtering/security policies and identity-based controls depend on enabled profiles, license and supported client/configuration.","Plan pilot internet traffic forwarding, define approved web/content filtering requirements, assign security profiles/policies as supported and test permitted and blocked categories/destinations. Inspect policies and actual traffic acquisition before rollout.","Review traffic logs and policy enforcement, compare intended internet traffic with private access and Microsoft 365 traffic. No broad production changes or paid activation.","What differs from Private Access?","Internet Access governs supported public internet traffic; Private Access connects authorized users to private apps.","Internet Access changes AD source anchor.","Private Access always replaces DNS."],[2,null,"The Microsoft traffic profile acquires supported Microsoft 365 traffic for GSA capabilities; other profiles govern private/internet traffic. Profile assignment and supported destinations determine acquisition, not a general right to every cloud app.","Select approved Microsoft traffic profile for pilot, configure supported client or remote network as applicable, inspect forwarding targets and test a representative Microsoft 365 request. Review actual workload sign-in/traffic context separately.","Verify traffic acquisition logs and intended CA/network signal where supported. If Teams works but acquisition is absent, check client/profile and supported destination rather than assuming policy enforcement.","Does enabling the Microsoft 365 profile automatically forward every private Payroll server?","No; private application segments and appropriate forwarding configuration are separate.","Yes, all IP traffic is always acquired.","Yes, if the user is a guest."]]];
